(runbook-authoring-manifest.json) is the persistent allocation ledger for the mock-fantasy-quest-4 authoring system. The JSON is authoritative for IDs, provenance, dependencies, output paths, counters, and recovery state. Accepted IDs are immutable: resume from the first non-accepted batch and append only at `B0007` and `R0013`.\nprojects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/planning/runbook-authoring-manifest.md:11:| 3 | B0003 | R0004, R0008 | After B0002 | Validate state slice and P0 service exclusion |\nprojects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/planning/runbook-authoring-manifest.md:16:The batches are deliberately smaller than seven where a contract is context-heavy (state, persistence, content) or tightly coupled to its predecessor. Together they form an incremental foundation vertical slice: reproducible baseline, shared seams/test harness, deterministic game state and P0 exclusion, content and recovery boundaries, then provenance/diagnostic controls and documented closure. They do not imply that the game is implemented.\nprojects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/planning/runbook-authoring-manifest.md:25:| R0004 | IMP-0004 | B0003 | `runbooks/R0004.md` | not applicable \u2014 state envelopes only |\nprojects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/planning/runbook-authoring-manifest.md:47:| SHARD-02 \u2014 Campaign content and domain logic | Wave 2 after GATE-0004 | queued |\nprojects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/planning/runbook-authoring-manifest.md:48:| SHARD-03 \u2014 Session, UI, input, and accessibility | Wave 3 after SHARD-02 and GATE-0002/0003/0004 | queued |\nprojects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/planning/runbook-authoring-manifest.md:49:| SHARD-04 \u2014 PWA delivery, performance, operations, release evidence | Wave 4 after SHARD-02/03 and GATE-0002/0005/0007 | queued |\n\nexec\n/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '1,260p' projects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/authoring-runbooks/B0006.md; sed -n '1,220p' projects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/planning/runbook-authoring-manifest.md; sed -n '1,160p' projects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/specification/decision-log.md; ls projects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/authoring-runbooks; git status --short\" in /Users/simon/Dev/github/jakowicz/runbook-generator\n succeeded in 0ms:\n---\ntask_id: B0006\nsequence: 7\ntitle: Author the foundation documentation closure contract\nbrowser_impact: not_applicable\nplaywright_spec:\n---\n\n## Objective\n\nAfter B0003 and B0004, create only `../runbooks/R0012.md` for IMP-0012. It\ndocuments foundation architecture/recovery boundaries without claiming product\nimplementation, approval, or release readiness.\n\n## Context packet\n\nRead `../PROJECT_BRIEF.md`, the B0006/R0012 manifest and catalogue records,\n`03-technical-contract.md#architecture-and-requirement-traceability`,\n`03-technical-contract.md#persistence-migration-and-failure-semantics`, and\n`03-technical-contract.md#trust-privacy-and-security`.\n\n## Output list\n\n- `../runbooks/R0012.md` \u2014 IMP-0012; dependencies R0002,R0004,R0006,R0008.\n\n## Required authoring content\n\nUse standard front matter with the exact manifest provenance (`authoring_batch:\nB0006`, `factory_stages: F011,F012,F013`), `asset_impact: not_applicable`, and\nblank `asset_ids`. Include objective, dependencies, requirements, ownership,\nrequired behaviour, non-goals, platform differences, verification/evidence,\nobservable criteria, and an `## Asset assessment` explaining why no asset is\nneeded and prohibiting incidental assets.\n\n## Validation procedure\n\nStop rather than overwrite a conflicting R0012. Load it, compare all metadata,\nprovenance, dependencies, and ownership to the manifest, and check every\nrequired section and explicit retained gate.\n\n## Checkpoint and update procedure\n\nUpdate B0006 only after validation. Retain the reserved ID and repair only this\nfile on resume.\n\n## Stop condition\n\nStop after R0012; do not write a final audit or allocate another R contract.\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n\n- R0012 is the only output and maps exactly to IMP-0012.\n- It is a bounded, loader-valid, fully traceable contract with a no-asset assessment.\n- A conflicting existing contract is reported without overwrite or ID reuse.\n- Checkpointing follows successful manifest comparison only.\n# Runbook-authoring allocation ledger\n\nThis readable companion to [`runbook-authoring-manifest.json`](runbook-authoring-manifest.json) is the persistent allocation ledger for the mock-fantasy-quest-4 authoring system. The JSON is authoritative for IDs, provenance, dependencies, output paths, counters, and recovery state. Accepted IDs are immutable: resume from the first non-accepted batch and append only at `B0007` and `R0013`.\n\n## Wave 1 \u2014 foundation contracts\n\n| Order | Batch | R contracts (maximum 7) | Dependency / parallel disposition | Checkpoint |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| 1 | B0001 | R0001 | GATE-0001; no parallel work | Record gate and R0001 ownership |\n| 2 | B0002 | R0002, R0003, R0010 | After B0001 | Validate seams, fixtures, tokens and retained gates |\n| 3 | B0003 | R0004, R0008 | After B0002 | Validate state slice and P0 service exclusion |\n| 4 | B0004 | R0005, R0006 | After B0003; R0006 waits for GATE-0003 | Validate content/save separation |\n| 5 | B0005 | R0007, R0009, R0011 | After B0004; R0007/GATE-0003 and R0009/GATE-0005 remain blocked | Validate migration, diagnostics, provenance |\n| 6 | B0006 | R0012 | After B0003 and B0004; safe in parallel with B0005 after B0004 | Cross-check foundation documentation and gates |\n\nThe batches are deliberately smaller than seven where a contract is context-heavy (state, persistence, content) or tightly coupled to its predecessor. Together they form an incremental foundation vertical slice: reproducible baseline, shared seams/test harness, deterministic game state and P0 exclusion, content and recovery boundaries, then provenance/diagnostic controls and documented closure. They do not imply that the game is implemented.\n\n## Contract allocation and asset assessment\n\n| R ID | IMP source | Writer | Output | Asset assessment |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| R0001 | IMP-0001 | B0001 | `runbooks/R0001.md` | not applicable \u2014 tooling boundary only |\n| R0002 | IMP-0002 | B0002 | `runbooks/R0002.md` | not applicable \u2014 interfaces only |\n| R0003 | IMP-0003 | B0002 | `runbooks/R0003.md` | not applicable \u2014 deterministic fixtures only |\n| R0004 | IMP-0004 | B0003 | `runbooks/R0004.md` | not applicable \u2014 state envelopes only |\n| R0005 | IMP-0005 | B0004 | `runbooks/R0005.md` | not applicable \u2014 schema reserves later asset fields |\n| R0006 | IMP-0006 | B0004 | `runbooks/R0006.md` | not applicable \u2014 save/recovery protocol |\n| R0007 | IMP-0007 | B0005 | `runbooks/R0007.md` | not applicable \u2014 migrations only |\n| R0008 | IMP-0008 | B0003 | `runbooks/R0008.md` | not applicable \u2014 negative service scope |\n| R0009 | IMP-0009 | B0005 | `runbooks/R0009.md` | not applicable \u2014 local diagnostics only |\n| R0010 | IMP-0010 | B0002 | `runbooks/R0010.md` | not applicable \u2014 no final art/fonts/icons |\n| R0011 | IMP-0011 | B0005 | `runbooks/R0011.md` | not applicable \u2014 provenance control only |\n| R0012 | IMP-0012 | B0006 | `runbooks/R0012.md` | not applicable \u2014 documentation only |\n\nNo Wave 1 contract creates a product asset. Each contract still carries an explicit `asset_impact`, empty `asset_ids`, and an asset brief in the JSON; its author must prohibit incidental asset creation. A later asset-required contract must reserve stable product-specific IDs and a brief before assignment.\n\n## Author packet and resume rules\n\nEach B writer receives only the baseline terminology, target constraints, templates, authoring rules, and the named specification/catalogue records in its JSON `context_packet`. Every R record has canonical `source_specifications`, `source_catalogue_ids`, `authoring_batch`, and `factory_stages` provenance. Use [`runbooks/TEMPLATE.md`](../../../runbooks/TEMPLATE.md) plus the F014 metadata and asset requirements.\n\nAt every checkpoint, validate unique B/R IDs, output-path ownership, dependency closure, provenance, the batch maximum, asset assessment, and retained human gates. On failure, retain all assigned IDs, repair the bounded failed output, and resume that same batch. Never regenerate accepted contracts or renumber the ledger.\n\n## Queued expansion chapters\n\n| Chapter | Future allocation point | State |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| SHARD-02 \u2014 Campaign content and domain logic | Wave 2 after GATE-0004 | queued |\n| SHARD-03 \u2014 Session, UI, input, and accessibility | Wave 3 after SHARD-02 and GATE-0002/0003/0004 | queued |\n| SHARD-04 \u2014 PWA delivery, performance, operations, release evidence | Wave 4 after SHARD-02/03 and GATE-0002/0005/0007 | queued |\n| SHARD-05 \u2014 Connected-scope decision package | Only explicit authorisation after GATE-0006; no P0 R allocation | hold |\n\nExpand one queued chapter into catalogue records before allocating its R IDs. This preserves the catalogue\u2019s dependency graph and makes a future thousand-runbook programme resumable without changing accepted work.\n\n## Structural check\n\n```sh\nnode -e 'const m=require(\"./projects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/planning/runbook-authoring-manifest.json\"); const r=m.r_contracts, b=m.batches, ids=a=>new Set(a).size===a.length; if (!ids(r.map(x=>x.id)) || !ids(b.map(x=>x.id)) || r.length!==m.expected_runbook_count || b.some(x=>x.r_contract_ids.length>7) || r.some(x=>!x.source_specifications.length || !x.source_catalogue_ids.length || !x.authoring_batch || !x.factory_stages.length || ![\"required\",\"not_applicable\"].includes(x.asset_impact) || !x.output_path || (x.asset_impact===\"required\" && (!x.asset_ids.length || !x.asset_brief))) ) process.exit(1); console.log(\"runbook authoring ledger structural check passed\")'\n```\n# Decision log\n\nAll items are unresolved; defaults are containment behaviours, not invented selections. **Owner** is the role authorised to decide, not a named person.\n\n| ID | Decision / owner / due point | Alternatives | Default until decided | Consequence |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| DEC-0001 | Framework, package manager, CI / technical owner / before DM-01 | approved stack choices | no stack selected | GATE-0001; cannot bootstrap |\n| DEC-0002 | Support/browser/device/AT/network matrix and numeric budgets / product+technical owner / before compatibility promise or DM-19 | approved target matrix/budgets | \u201cwidely used\u201d is not a claim | GATE-0002; performance informational only |\n| DEC-0003 | Storage API/quota, save window/export/recovery support / technical+product owner / before DM-05 | approved local storage policies | no quota/export promise; preserve safe fallback | GATE-0003 |\n| DEC-0004 | Party/content quantities, balance, locale, audio/remapping, font/contrast / game+experience owner / before DM-04/16 | approved production choices | one bounded campaign only; no numeric/content promise | GATE-0004 |\n| DEC-0005 | Hosting/CDN, cache rollout/rollback, diagnostic transport/retention, support owner / delivery+privacy owner / before DM-17/18/release | approved operating model | local/no-network diagnostics; no host selected | GATE-0005 |\n| DEC-0006 | Identity/sync, multiplayer/safety, live service, IAP/entitlements / product owner / before any related implementation | exclude; or approve scoped service contracts | excluded from P0 | GATE-0006; protects offline campaign |\n| DEC-0007 | Ratings, age, region, legal/store/certification policy / product/legal owner / before distribution claim | applicable approved policy | no certification/legal claim | GATE-0007 |\nB0001.md\nB0002.md\nB0003.md\nB0004.md\nB0005.md\nB0006.md\nC0001.md\nD0001.md\nINITIAL.md\n M README.md\n M scripts/runbookgen_status.py\n m supervisor\n?? projects/mock-fantasy-quest-2/\n?? projects/mock-fantasy-quest-3/\n?? projects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/\n?? projects/mock-fantasy-quest/\n?? runbooks/.supervisor-children/\n\ncodex\n